Town_Hall_001.JPG/960px-Port_Melbourne_Town_Hall_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hobsons Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.854722,"lo":144.935833,"d":1326,"ds":"Hobsons Bay is a small open bay in Victoria, Australia, and is the northernmost part of the larger Port Phillip Bay. Its western and eastern boundaries are marked by Point Gellibrand in Williamstown and Point Ormond in Elwood respectively, and defines the coastal margin of the Melbourne suburbs of Williamstown, Newport, Port Melbourne, Albert Park, Middle Park, St. Kilda West, St.","wpu":"Hobsons Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Williamstown_-_Hobsons_Bay_Yacht_Club_%28Eastablished_in_1888%29.jpg/960px-Williamstown_-_Hobsons_Bay_Yacht_Club_%28Eastablished_in_1888%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Garden City, Victoria","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8362,"lo":144.9199,"d":1376,"ds":"Garden City is a residential area of Melbourne located in the most westerly part of Port Melbourne, 5km south-west of the Melbourne CBD, generally bounded by what is now Williamstown Road, Howe Parade, and Walter Street. It was the first housing estate created by a public authority in Victoria, developed in stages by the State Bank of Victoria between 1926 and 1948.","wpu":"Garden City, Victoria","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Garden_City%2C_Port_Melbourne_in_1929.jpg/960px-Garden_City%2C_Port_Melbourne_in_1929.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Albert Park, Victoria","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.842,"lo":144.95,"d":1496,"ds":"Albert Park is an inner suburb of Melbourne, Victoria, Australia, 4 km (2.5 mi) south of Melbourne's Central Business District. The suburb is named after Albert Park, a large lakeside urban park located within the City of Port Phillip local government area. Albert Park recorded a population of 6,044 at the 2021 census.","wpu":"Albert Park, Victoria","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Port_Melbourne_Bayside_Foreshore_Promenade.jpg/960px-Port_Melbourne_Bayside_Foreshore_Promenade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St Vincent Gardens","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8389,"lo":144.9555,"d":2027,"ds":"St Vincent Gardens is a 3.4-hectare (8.4-acre) garden square set in a Victorian Georgian residential development located in Albert Park, an inner-city suburb of Melbourne, Victoria, Australia. Envisaged in the 1850s, the park and dwellings were designed by Clement Hodgkinson in the 1860s. The park and surrounding streets are located within the City of Port Phillip.","wpu":"St Vincent Gardens","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/St_vincent_garden_albert_park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Park Towers (South Melbourne)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8357,"lo":144.9577,"d":2315,"ds":"Park Towers is a 31-storey public housing tower in South Melbourne, Victoria built 1967–69. It is historically and architecturally the most significant of the 47 public housing towers in Victoria. At the time of its completion in 1969, the building was believed to be the tallest pre-cast load-bearing wall building in the world and had more storeys than any other building in Melbourne.","wpu":"Park Towers (South Melbourne)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/South_Melbourne_Housing_Commission_Flats.JPG/960px-South_Melbourne_Housing_Commission_Flats.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yarra River","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.851944,"lo":144.908333,"d":2383,"ds":"The Yarra River or historically, the Yarra Yarra River, (Kulin languages: Berrern, Birr-arrung, Bay-ray-rung, Birarang, Birrarung, and Wongete) is a perennial river in south-central Victoria, Australia. The lower stretches of the Yarra are where Victoria's state capital Melbourne was established in 1835, and today metropolitan Greater Melbourne dominates and influences the landscape of its lower reaches.","wpu":"Yarra River","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/AUS_Melbourne%2C_Melbourne%2C_Queensbridge_Square%2C_View_002.jpg/960px-AUS_Melbourne%2C_Melbourne%2C_Queensbridge_Square%2C_View_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"South Melbourne Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8321,"lo":144.9565,"d":2393,"ds":"The South Melbourne Market is a covered food and general goods market located in South Melbourne, Victoria. Opened in 1867, it is the oldest continuously operating market in Melbourne.","wpu":"South Melbourne Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/South_Melbourne_market_outside_1a.jpg/960px-South_Melbourne_market_outside_1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Carmelite Library","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.848453,"lo":144.960213,"d":2465,"ds":"The Carmelite Library is an academic theological library located in Middle Park, Victoria, Australia. The library specialises in the areas of Carmelite Studies, Mariology, Spirituality and Mysticism, and holds books and journals in these subject areas.","wpu":"Carmelite Library","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Carmelite_Library.jpg/960px-Carmelite_Library.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Planetshakers Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.829328,"lo":144.95532,"d":2480,"ds":"Planetshakers Church is a Pentecostal megachurch affiliated with Australian Christian Churches (Assemblies of God) based in Melbourne, Australia, with several church campuses around the world.","wpu":"Planetshakers Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Planetshakers_Church.jpg/960px-Planetshakers_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Charles Grimes Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.823333,"lo":144.948056,"d":2555,"ds":"The Charles Grimes Bridge is a dual-carriageway road bridge that carries the Docklands Highway across the Yarra River, located adjacent to the city centre of Melbourne, in Victoria, Australia. The bridge links the centre centre in the north with the Docklands precinct in the south. It was named after New South Wales surveyor general Charles Grimes, the first European to see the Yarra River.","wpu":"Charles Grimes Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Panorama_of_the_Charles_Grimes_Bridge_over_the_Yarra_River_%281%29.jpg/960px-Panorama_of_the_Charles_Grimes_Bridge_over_the_Yarra_River_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Melbourne Sports and Aquatic Centre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.843111,"lo":144.962167,"d":2561,"ds":"Melbourne Sports and Aquatic Centre (MSAC) is an international sporting venue located in Albert Park, Victoria, Australia. The centre was opened on 24 July 1997 at a construction cost of A$65 million. The cost was funded by the State Government of Victoria and the City of Port Phillip.","wpu":"Melbourne Sports and Aquatic Centre","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Olympic_Swimming_Pool.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Bolte Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.819444,"lo":144.932222,"d":2620,"ds":"The Bolte Bridge is a twin cantilever road bridge across the Yarra River and Victoria Harbour, located in Melbourne, Victoria, Australia. The bridge carries the Melbourne CityLink (Western Link) via eight lanes of traffic – four lanes northbound and four lanes southbound.","wpu":"Bolte Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Bolte_Bridge%2C_Melbourne_2017-10-28.jpg/960px-Bolte_Bridge%2C_Melbourne_2017-10-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"See Yup temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8368,"lo":144.9631,"d":2731,"ds":"The See Yup Temple (Chinese: 四邑關帝廟) is a heritage-listed Chinese temple located at 76 Raglan Street, South Melbourne, Victoria, Australia. The current building was erected in 1866 for the See Yup Society, as the principal centre of worship and death registry of descendants from the See Yup area in Victoria. The temple is dedicated to Kuan Ti.","wpu":"See Yup temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/See_Yup_Temple_-_Melbourne_10_Dec_2005.jpg/960px-See_Yup_Temple_-_Melbourne_10_Dec_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Victoria Dock (Melbourne)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.818042,"lo":144.935562,"d":2784,"ds":"Victoria Dock also known as Victoria Harbour is a large excavated harbour basin located off Footscray Road and Docklands Highway, Docklands, in Melbourne. It was constructed between 1887 and 1892, under the supervision of Melbourne Harbor Trust engineer Joseph Brady, to modified designs of British engineer Sir John Coode. It is the oldest and largest surviving single dock in the world.","wpu":"Victoria Dock (Melbourne)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Victoria_docks_melbourne_in_the_1920s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Middle Park, Victoria","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.851944,"lo":144.963056,"d":2820,"ds":"Middle Park is an inner suburb in Melbourne, Victoria, Australia, 5 km south of Melbourne's Central Business District, located within the City of Port Phillip local government area. Middle Park recorded a population of 4,000 at the 2021 census.. The average house price is approximately $2.8 million.","wpu":"Middle Park, Victoria","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Middle_Park_Hotel.jpg/960px-Middle_Park_Hotel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"HMAS Lonsdale (naval base)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.823889,"lo":144.911111,"d":2865,"ds":"HMAS Lonsdale is a former Royal Australian Navy (RAN) training base that was located at Beach Street, Port Melbourne, Victoria, Australia. Originally named Cerberus III, the Naval Reserve Base was commissioned as HMAS Lonsdale on 1 August 1940 during World War II. Lonsdale was decommissioned in 1992, and the site now houses a luxury apartment complex known as HM@S Lonsdale, designed by Fender Katsalidis Architects.","wpu":"HMAS Lonsdale (naval base)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Prayers_at_Divisions_-_Rex_Julius.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Victoria Police Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8225,"lo":144.954111,"d":2938,"ds":"The Victoria Police Museum is a law enforcement museum operated by the Historical Services Unit within Victoria Police. The new state-of-the-art museum reopened to the public on Monday 25 September 2023, following a 3 year build and development.","wpu":"Victoria Police Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Victoria_Police_Museum.jpg/960px-Victoria_Police_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Victoria Point (building)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.818611,"lo":144.946944,"d":2975,"ds":"Victoria Point is a 42 level residential tower located at the corner of Bourke Street and Harbour Esplanade in Docklands, a suburb of Melbourne, Australia. The site on which Victoria Point sits was originally controlled by the consortium (including Channel 7 and Baulderstone Hornibrook) which developed the Docklands Stadium.","wpu":"Victoria Point (building)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Victoria_Point_Docklands.jpg/960px-Victoria_Point_Docklands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pier 35 (Port Melbourne)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-37.8237,"lo":144.9073,"d":3114,"ds":"Pier 35 is a pier along Lorimer Street on the Yarra River in Port Melbourne, Victoria, Australia. It contains the Pier 35 Bar and Grill restaurant, formerly the Steakhouse, and d'Albora Marinas, a major supplier of marina berths. In 2004, Australia's leading marina owner, Macquarie Leisure, purchased Pier 35 for almost $A15 million.","wpu":"Pier 35 (Port Melbourne)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/Pier35_marina_fishermans_bend.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Melbourne ( MEL-bərn, locally [ˈmæɫbən] ; Boonwurrung/Woiwurrung: Narrm or Naarm) is the capital and most populous city of the Australian state of Victoria and the second-most populous city in Australia. The city's name generally refers to a 9,993-square-kilometre (3,858 sq mi) area, comprising an urban agglomeration of 30 local government areas. The name is also used specifically to refer to the local government area, also named the City of Melbourne, whose administrative area is centred on the Melbourne central business district and some immediate surrounds.","wpu":"Melbourne","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Melbourne_skyline_sor.jpg/960px-Melbourne_skyline_sor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}